# Every word this engine implements, and therefore every place the data delegates to the code.
#
# **This is the boundary the prototype is for.** A row of `clause` or `binding` is data the engine
# moves around without knowing what it means - a second rule is rows and no code. A word in this
# file is the other thing: the engine holds the string and branches on it, so a word that is not
# here cannot be added without writing Rust.
#
# **Three kinds are mixed together and the difference is worth seeing.** Some are column names the
# engine reads by name - `name`, `seq`, `id`. Some are relation names it dispatches on - `clause`,
# `load`. And some are values in a row that select a code path - `require`, `remove`, `add`, which
# is the purest case: `{role name:require}` is a row whose entire meaning is a match arm.
#
# **Generated from the constants in `src/` and checked against them both ways** by
# `tests/engine.rs`: a match arm with no row here fails, and a row here with no match arm fails.
# **So this list cannot drift**, which is the whole reason it is worth having rather than writing
# the same thing in a comment.
#
# WHY: `primitive` is a name rather than a decision - `builtin` or `implemented` would do. And
# nothing reads these rows at run time; they exist to be checked.

{relation name:primitive}
{column id:primitive.word relation:primitive seq:1 name:word}

{primitive word:actual}
{primitive word:add}
{primitive word:argument}
{primitive word:binding}
{primitive word:clause}
{primitive word:column}
{primitive word:command}
{primitive word:compare}
{primitive word:execute}
{primitive word:expected}
{primitive word:file}
{primitive word:game}
{primitive word:id}
{primitive word:input}
{primitive word:into}
{primitive word:load}
{primitive word:name}
{primitive word:of}
{primitive word:reference}
{primitive word:relation}
{primitive word:remove}
{primitive word:report}
{primitive word:require}
{primitive word:role}
{primitive word:rule}
{primitive word:script}
{primitive word:seq}
{primitive word:state}
{primitive word:test}
{primitive word:this}
{primitive word:title}
{primitive word:to}
{primitive word:value}
{primitive word:with}
